{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_47","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_13-42-19-etcd_raft-real-run/agent-source && sed -n '150,400p' raft.go | nl -ba -v150\"","aggregated_output":"   150\t\t// applied entries. This is a very application dependent configuration.\n   151\t\tApplied uint64\n   152\t\n   153\t\t// AsyncStorageWrites configures the raft node to write to its local storage\n   154\t\t// (raft log and state machine) using a request/response message passing\n   155\t\t// interface instead of the default Ready/Advance function call interface.\n   156\t\t// Local storage messages can be pipelined and processed asynchronously\n   157\t\t// (with respect to Ready iteration), facilitating reduced interference\n   158\t\t// between Raft proposals and increased batching of log appends and state\n   159\t\t// machine application. As a result, use of asynchronous storage writes can\n   160\t\t// reduce end-to-end commit latency and increase maximum throughput.\n   161\t\t//\n   162\t\t// When true, the Ready.Message slice will include MsgStorageAppend and\n   163\t\t// MsgStorageApply messages. The messages will target a LocalAppendThread\n   164\t\t// and a LocalApplyThread, respectively. Messages to the same target must be\n   165\t\t// reliably processed in order. In other words, they can't be dropped (like\n   166\t\t// messages over the network) and those targeted at the same thread can't be\n   167\t\t// reordered. Messages to different targets can be processed in any order.\n   168\t\t//\n   169\t\t// MsgStorageAppend carries Raft log entries to append, election votes /\n   170\t\t// term changes / updated commit indexes to persist, and snapshots to apply.\n   171\t\t// All writes performed in service of a MsgStorageAppend must be durable\n   172\t\t// before response messages are delivered. However, if the MsgStorageAppend\n   173\t\t// carries no response messages, durability is not required. The message\n   174\t\t// assumes the role of the Entries, HardState, and Snapshot fields in Ready.\n   175\t\t//\n   176\t\t// MsgStorageApply carries committed entries to apply. Writes performed in\n   177\t\t// service of a MsgStorageApply need not be durable before response messages\n   178\t\t// are delivered. The message assumes the role of the CommittedEntries field\n   179\t\t// in Ready.\n   180\t\t//\n   181\t\t// Local messages each carry one or more response messages which should be\n   182\t\t// delivered after the corresponding storage write has been completed. These\n   183\t\t// responses may target the same node or may target other nodes. The storage\n   184\t\t// threads are not responsible for understanding the response messages, only\n   185\t\t// for delivering them to the correct target after performing the storage\n   186\t\t// write.\n   187\t\tAsyncStorageWrites bool\n   188\t\n   189\t\t// MaxSizePerMsg limits the max byte size of each append message. Smaller\n   190\t\t// value lowers the raft recovery cost(initial probing and message lost\n   191\t\t// during normal operation). On the other side, it might affect the\n   192\t\t// throughput during normal replication. Note: math.MaxUint64 for unlimited,\n   193\t\t// 0 for at most one entry per message.\n   194\t\tMaxSizePerMsg uint64\n   195\t\t// MaxCommittedSizePerReady limits the size of the committed entries which\n   196\t\t// can be applying at the same time.\n   197\t\t//\n   198\t\t// Despite its name (preserved for compatibility), this quota applies across\n   199\t\t// Ready structs to encompass all outstanding entries in unacknowledged\n   200\t\t// MsgStorageApply messages when AsyncStorageWrites is enabled.\n   201\t\tMaxCommittedSizePerReady uint64\n   202\t\t// MaxUncommittedEntriesSize limits the aggregate byte size of the\n   203\t\t// uncommitted entries that may be appended to a leader's log. Once this\n   204\t\t// limit is exceeded, proposals will begin to return ErrProposalDropped\n   205\t\t// errors. Note: 0 for no limit.\n   206\t\tMaxUncommittedEntriesSize uint64\n   207\t\t// MaxInflightMsgs limits the max number of in-flight append messages during\n   208\t\t// optimistic replication phase. The application transportation layer usually\n   209\t\t// has its own sending buffer over TCP/UDP. Setting MaxInflightMsgs to avoid\n   210\t\t// overflowing that sending buffer. TODO (xiangli): feedback to application to\n   211\t\t// limit the proposal rate?\n   212\t\tMaxInflightMsgs int\n   213\t\t// MaxInflightBytes limits the number of in-flight bytes in append messages.\n   214\t\t// Complements MaxInflightMsgs. Ignored if zero.\n   215\t\t//\n   216\t\t// This effectively bounds the bandwidth-delay product. Note that especially\n   217\t\t// in high-latency deployments setting this too low can lead to a dramatic\n   218\t\t// reduction in throughput. For example, with a peer that has a round-trip\n   219\t\t// latency of 100ms to the leader and this setting is set to 1 MB, there is a\n   220\t\t// throughput limit of 10 MB/s for this group. With RTT of 400ms, this drops\n   221\t\t// to 2.5 MB/s. See Little's law to understand the maths behind.\n   222\t\tMaxInflightBytes uint64\n   223\t\n   224\t\t// CheckQuorum specifies if the leader should check quorum activity. Leader\n   225\t\t// steps down when quorum is not active for an electionTimeout.\n   226\t\tCheckQuorum bool\n   227\t\n   228\t\t// PreVote enables the Pre-Vote algorithm described in raft thesis section\n   229\t\t// 9.6. This prevents disruption when a node that has been partitioned away\n   230\t\t// rejoins the cluster.\n   231\t\tPreVote bool\n   232\t\n   233\t\t// ReadOnlyOption specifies how the read only request is processed.\n   234\t\t//\n   235\t\t// ReadOnlySafe guarantees the linearizability of the read only request by\n   236\t\t// communicating with the quorum. It is the default and suggested option.\n   237\t\t//\n   238\t\t// ReadOnlyLeaseBased ensures linearizability of the read only request by\n   239\t\t// relying on the leader lease. It can be affected by clock drift.\n   240\t\t// If the clock drift is unbounded, leader might keep the lease longer than it\n   241\t\t// should (clock can move backward/pause without any bound). ReadIndex is not safe\n   242\t\t// in that case.\n   243\t\t// CheckQuorum MUST be enabled if ReadOnlyOption is ReadOnlyLeaseBased.\n   244\t\tReadOnlyOption ReadOnlyOption\n   245\t\n   246\t\t// Logger is the logger used for raft log. For multinode which can host\n   247\t\t// multiple raft group, each raft group can have its own logger\n   248\t\tLogger Logger\n   249\t\n   250\t\t// DisableProposalForwarding set to true means that followers will drop\n   251\t\t// proposals, rather than forwarding them to the leader. One use case for\n   252\t\t// this feature would be in a situation where the Raft leader is used to\n   253\t\t// compute the data of a proposal, for example, adding a timestamp from a\n   254\t\t// hybrid logical clock to data in a monotonically increasing way. Forwarding\n   255\t\t// should be disabled to prevent a follower with an inaccurate hybrid\n   256\t\t// logical clock from assigning the timestamp and then forwarding the data\n   257\t\t// to the leader.\n   258\t\tDisableProposalForwarding bool\n   259\t\n   260\t\t// DisableConfChangeValidation turns off propose-time verification of\n   261\t\t// configuration changes against the currently active configuration of the\n   262\t\t// raft instance. These checks are generally sensible (cannot leave a joint\n   263\t\t// config unless in a joint config, et cetera) but they have false positives\n   264\t\t// because the active configuration may not be the most recent\n   265\t\t// configuration. This is because configurations are activated during log\n   266\t\t// application, and even the leader can trail log application by an\n   267\t\t// unbounded number of entries.\n   268\t\t// Symmetrically, the mechanism has false negatives - because the check may\n   269\t\t// not run against the \"actual\" config that will be the predecessor of the\n   270\t\t// newly proposed one, the check may pass but the new config may be invalid\n   271\t\t// when it is being applied. In other words, the checks are best-effort.\n   272\t\t//\n   273\t\t// Users should *not* use this option unless they have a reliable mechanism\n   274\t\t// (above raft) that serializes and verifies configuration changes. If an\n   275\t\t// invalid configuration change enters the log and gets applied, a panic\n   276\t\t// will result.\n   277\t\t//\n   278\t\t// This option may be removed once false positives are no longer possible.\n   279\t\t// See: https://github.com/etcd-io/raft/issues/80\n   280\t\tDisableConfChangeValidation bool\n   281\t\n   282\t\t// StepDownOnRemoval makes the leader step down when it is removed from the\n   283\t\t// group or demoted to a learner.\n   284\t\t//\n   285\t\t// This behavior will become unconditional in the future. See:\n   286\t\t// https://github.com/etcd-io/raft/issues/83\n   287\t\tStepDownOnRemoval bool\n   288\t\n   289\t\t// raft state tracer\n   290\t\tTraceLogger TraceLogger\n   291\t}\n   292\t\n   293\tfunc (c *Config) validate() error {\n   294\t\tif c.ID == None {\n   295\t\t\treturn errors.New(\"cannot use none as id\")\n   296\t\t}\n   297\t\tif IsLocalMsgTarget(c.ID) {\n   298\t\t\treturn errors.New(\"cannot use local target as id\")\n   299\t\t}\n   300\t\n   301\t\tif c.HeartbeatTick <= 0 {\n   302\t\t\treturn errors.New(\"heartbeat tick must be greater than 0\")\n   303\t\t}\n   304\t\n   305\t\tif c.ElectionTick <= c.HeartbeatTick {\n   306\t\t\treturn errors.New(\"election tick must be greater than heartbeat tick\")\n   307\t\t}\n   308\t\n   309\t\tif c.Storage == nil {\n   310\t\t\treturn errors.New(\"storage cannot be nil\")\n   311\t\t}\n   312\t\n   313\t\tif c.MaxUncommittedEntriesSize == 0 {\n   314\t\t\tc.MaxUncommittedEntriesSize = noLimit\n   315\t\t}\n   316\t\n   317\t\t// default MaxCommittedSizePerReady to MaxSizePerMsg because they were\n   318\t\t// previously the same parameter.\n   319\t\tif c.MaxCommittedSizePerReady == 0 {\n   320\t\t\tc.MaxCommittedSizePerReady = c.MaxSizePerMsg\n   321\t\t}\n   322\t\n   323\t\tif c.MaxInflightMsgs <= 0 {\n   324\t\t\treturn errors.New(\"max inflight messages must be greater than 0\")\n   325\t\t}\n   326\t\tif c.MaxInflightBytes == 0 {\n   327\t\t\tc.MaxInflightBytes = noLimit\n   328\t\t} else if c.MaxInflightBytes < c.MaxSizePerMsg {\n   329\t\t\treturn errors.New(\"max inflight bytes must be >= max message size\")\n   330\t\t}\n   331\t\n   332\t\tif c.Logger == nil {\n   333\t\t\tc.Logger = getLogger()\n   334\t\t}\n   335\t\n   336\t\tif c.ReadOnlyOption == ReadOnlyLeaseBased && !c.CheckQuorum {\n   337\t\t\treturn errors.New(\"CheckQuorum must be enabled when ReadOnlyOption is ReadOnlyLeaseBased\")\n   338\t\t}\n   339\t\n   340\t\treturn nil\n   341\t}\n   342\t\n   343\ttype raft struct {\n   344\t\tid uint64\n   345\t\n   346\t\tTerm uint64\n   347\t\tVote uint64\n   348\t\n   349\t\treadStates []ReadState\n   350\t\n   351\t\t// the log\n   352\t\traftLog *raftLog\n   353\t\n   354\t\tmaxMsgSize         entryEncodingSize\n   355\t\tmaxUncommittedSize entryPayloadSize\n   356\t\n   357\t\ttrk tracker.ProgressTracker\n   358\t\n   359\t\tstate StateType\n   360\t\n   361\t\t// isLearner is true if the local raft node is a learner.\n   362\t\tisLearner bool\n   363\t\n   364\t\t// msgs contains the list of messages that should be sent out immediately to\n   365\t\t// other nodes.\n   366\t\t//\n   367\t\t// Messages in this list must target other nodes.\n   368\t\tmsgs []*pb.Message\n   369\t\t// msgsAfterAppend contains the list of messages that should be sent after\n   370\t\t// the accumulated unstable state (e.g. term, vote, []entry, and snapshot)\n   371\t\t// has been persisted to durable storage. This includes waiting for any\n   372\t\t// unstable state that is already in the process of being persisted (i.e.\n   373\t\t// has already been handed out in a prior Ready struct) to complete.\n   374\t\t//\n   375\t\t// Messages in this list may target other nodes or may target this node.\n   376\t\t//\n   377\t\t// Messages in this list have the type MsgAppResp, MsgVoteResp, or\n   378\t\t// MsgPreVoteResp. See the comment in raft.send for details.\n   379\t\tmsgsAfterAppend []*pb.Message\n   380\t\n   381\t\t// the leader id\n   382\t\tlead uint64\n   383\t\t// leadTransferee is id of the leader transfer target when its value is not zero.\n   384\t\t// Follow the procedure defined in raft thesis 3.10.\n   385\t\tleadTransferee uint64\n   386\t\t// Only one conf change may be pending (in the log, but not yet\n   387\t\t// applied) at a time. This is enforced via pendingConfIndex, which\n   388\t\t// is set to a value >= the log index of the latest pending\n   389\t\t// configuration change (if any). Config changes are only allowed to\n   390\t\t// be proposed if the leader's applied index is greater than this\n   391\t\t// value.\n   392\t\tpendingConfIndex uint64\n   393\t\t// disableConfChangeValidation is Config.DisableConfChangeValidation,\n   394\t\t// see there for details.\n   395\t\tdisableConfChangeValidation bool\n   396\t\t// an estimate of the size of the uncommitted tail of the Raft log. Used to\n   397\t\t// prevent unbounded log growth. Only maintained by the leader. Reset on\n   398\t\t// term changes.\n   399\t\tuncommittedSize entryPayloadSize\n   400\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_48","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_13-42-19-etcd_raft-real-run/agent-source && cat -n storage.go | sed -n '60,300p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_48","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_13-42-19-etcd_raft-real-run/agent-source && cat -n storage.go | sed -n '60,300p'\"","aggregated_output":"    60\t\t// returned, but Entries returns at least one entry if any.\n    61\t\t//\n    62\t\t// The caller of Entries owns the returned slice, and may append to it. The\n    63\t\t// individual entries in the slice must not be mutated, neither by the Storage\n    64\t\t// implementation nor the caller. Note that raft may forward these entries\n    65\t\t// back to the application via Ready struct, so the corresponding handler must\n    66\t\t// not mutate entries either (see comments in Ready struct).\n    67\t\t//\n    68\t\t// Since the caller may append to the returned slice, Storage implementation\n    69\t\t// must protect its state from corruption that such appends may cause. For\n    70\t\t// example, common ways to do so are:\n    71\t\t//  - allocate the slice before returning it (safest option),\n    72\t\t//  - return a slice protected by Go full slice expression, which causes\n    73\t\t//  copying on appends (see MemoryStorage).\n    74\t\t//\n    75\t\t// Returns ErrCompacted if entry lo has been compacted, or ErrUnavailable if\n    76\t\t// encountered an unavailable entry in [lo, hi).\n    77\t\tEntries(lo, hi, maxSize uint64) ([]*pb.Entry, error)\n    78\t\n    79\t\t// Term returns the term of entry i, which must be in the range\n    80\t\t// [FirstIndex()-1, LastIndex()]. The term of the entry before\n    81\t\t// FirstIndex is retained for matching purposes even though the\n    82\t\t// rest of that entry may not be available.\n    83\t\tTerm(i uint64) (uint64, error)\n    84\t\t// LastIndex returns the index of the last entry in the log.\n    85\t\tLastIndex() (uint64, error)\n    86\t\t// FirstIndex returns the index of the first log entry that is\n    87\t\t// possibly available via Entries (older entries have been incorporated\n    88\t\t// into the latest Snapshot; if storage only contains the dummy entry the\n    89\t\t// first log entry is not available).\n    90\t\tFirstIndex() (uint64, error)\n    91\t\t// Snapshot returns the most recent snapshot.\n    92\t\t// If snapshot is temporarily unavailable, it should return ErrSnapshotTemporarilyUnavailable,\n    93\t\t// so raft state machine could know that Storage needs some time to prepare\n    94\t\t// snapshot and call Snapshot later.\n    95\t\tSnapshot() (*pb.Snapshot, error)\n    96\t}\n    97\t\n    98\ttype inMemStorageCallStats struct {\n    99\t\tinitialState, firstIndex, lastIndex, entries, term, snapshot int\n   100\t}\n   101\t\n   102\t// MemoryStorage implements the Storage interface backed by an\n   103\t// in-memory array.\n   104\ttype MemoryStorage struct {\n   105\t\t// Protects access to all fields. Most methods of MemoryStorage are\n   106\t\t// run on the raft goroutine, but Append() is run on an application\n   107\t\t// goroutine.\n   108\t\tsync.Mutex\n   109\t\n   110\t\thardState *pb.HardState\n   111\t\tsnapshot  *pb.Snapshot\n   112\t\t// ents[i] has raft log position i+ms.snapshot.GetMetadata().GetIndex()\n   113\t\tents []*pb.Entry\n   114\t\n   115\t\tcallStats inMemStorageCallStats\n   116\t}\n   117\t\n   118\t// NewMemoryStorage creates an empty MemoryStorage.\n   119\tfunc NewMemoryStorage() *MemoryStorage {\n   120\t\tms := &MemoryStorage{\n   121\t\t\t// When starting from scratch populate the list with a dummy entry at term zero.\n   122\t\t\tents: []*pb.Entry{{}},\n   123\t\t}\n   124\t\tms.snapshot = pb.EnsureSnapshot(ms.snapshot)\n   125\t\treturn ms\n   126\t}\n   127\t\n   128\t// InitialState implements the Storage interface.\n   129\tfunc (ms *MemoryStorage) InitialState() (*pb.HardState, *pb.ConfState, error) {\n   130\t\tms.callStats.initialState++\n   131\t\tcs := ms.snapshot.GetMetadata().GetConfState()\n   132\t\tcs = pb.EnsureConfState(cs)\n   133\t\treturn ms.hardState, cs, nil\n   134\t}\n   135\t\n   136\t// SetHardState saves the current HardState.\n   137\tfunc (ms *MemoryStorage) SetHardState(st *pb.HardState) error {\n   138\t\tms.Lock()\n   139\t\tdefer ms.Unlock()\n   140\t\tms.hardState = st\n   141\t\treturn nil\n   142\t}\n   143\t\n   144\t// Entries implements the Storage interface.\n   145\tfunc (ms *MemoryStorage) Entries(lo, hi, maxSize uint64) ([]*pb.Entry, error) {\n   146\t\tms.Lock()\n   147\t\tdefer ms.Unlock()\n   148\t\tms.callStats.entries++\n   149\t\toffset := ms.ents[0].GetIndex()\n   150\t\tif lo <= offset {\n   151\t\t\treturn nil, ErrCompacted\n   152\t\t}\n   153\t\tif hi > ms.lastIndex()+1 {\n   154\t\t\tgetLogger().Panicf(\"entries' hi(%d) is out of bound lastindex(%d)\", hi, ms.lastIndex())\n   155\t\t}\n   156\t\t// only contains dummy entries.\n   157\t\tif len(ms.ents) == 1 {\n   158\t\t\treturn nil, ErrUnavailable\n   159\t\t}\n   160\t\n   161\t\tents := limitSize(ms.ents[lo-offset:hi-offset], entryEncodingSize(maxSize))\n   162\t\t// NB: use the full slice expression to limit what the caller can do with the\n   163\t\t// returned slice. For example, an append will reallocate and copy this slice\n   164\t\t// instead of corrupting the neighbouring ms.ents.\n   165\t\treturn ents[:len(ents):len(ents)], nil\n   166\t}\n   167\t\n   168\t// Term implements the Storage interface.\n   169\tfunc (ms *MemoryStorage) Term(i uint64) (uint64, error) {\n   170\t\tms.Lock()\n   171\t\tdefer ms.Unlock()\n   172\t\tms.callStats.term++\n   173\t\toffset := ms.ents[0].GetIndex()\n   174\t\tif i < offset {\n   175\t\t\treturn 0, ErrCompacted\n   176\t\t}\n   177\t\tif int(i-offset) >= len(ms.ents) {\n   178\t\t\treturn 0, ErrUnavailable\n   179\t\t}\n   180\t\treturn ms.ents[i-offset].GetTerm(), nil\n   181\t}\n   182\t\n   183\t// LastIndex implements the Storage interface.\n   184\tfunc (ms *MemoryStorage) LastIndex() (uint64, error) {\n   185\t\tms.Lock()\n   186\t\tdefer ms.Unlock()\n   187\t\tms.callStats.lastIndex++\n   188\t\treturn ms.lastIndex(), nil\n   189\t}\n   190\t\n   191\tfunc (ms *MemoryStorage) lastIndex() uint64 {\n   192\t\treturn ms.ents[0].GetIndex() + uint64(len(ms.ents)) - 1\n   193\t}\n   194\t\n   195\t// FirstIndex implements the Storage interface.\n   196\tfunc (ms *MemoryStorage) FirstIndex() (uint64, error) {\n   197\t\tms.Lock()\n   198\t\tdefer ms.Unlock()\n   199\t\tms.callStats.firstIndex++\n   200\t\treturn ms.firstIndex(), nil\n   201\t}\n   202\t\n   203\tfunc (ms *MemoryStorage) firstIndex() uint64 {\n   204\t\treturn ms.ents[0].GetIndex() + 1\n   205\t}\n   206\t\n   207\t// Snapshot implements the Storage interface.\n   208\tfunc (ms *MemoryStorage) Snapshot() (*pb.Snapshot, error) {\n   209\t\tms.Lock()\n   210\t\tdefer ms.Unlock()\n   211\t\tms.callStats.snapshot++\n   212\t\tms.snapshot = pb.EnsureSnapshot(ms.snapshot)\n   213\t\treturn proto.Clone(ms.snapshot).(*pb.Snapshot), nil\n   214\t}\n   215\t\n   216\t// ApplySnapshot overwrites the contents of this Storage object with\n   217\t// those of the given snapshot.\n   218\tfunc (ms *MemoryStorage) ApplySnapshot(snap *pb.Snapshot) error {\n   219\t\tms.Lock()\n   220\t\tdefer ms.Unlock()\n   221\t\n   222\t\tsnap = pb.EnsureSnapshot(snap)\n   223\t\n   224\t\t//handle check for old snapshot being applied\n   225\t\tmsIndex := ms.snapshot.GetMetadata().GetIndex()\n   226\t\tsnapIndex := snap.GetMetadata().GetIndex()\n   227\t\t// During bootstrap, applications (e.g., etcd) may initialize only the\n   228\t\t// ConfState in the snapshot. In this case, both the snapshot index and\n   229\t\t// term are 0.\n   230\t\tif msIndex != 0 && msIndex >= snapIndex {\n   231\t\t\treturn ErrSnapOutOfDate\n   232\t\t}\n   233\t\n   234\t\tms.snapshot = proto.Clone(snap).(*pb.Snapshot)\n   235\t\tms.ents = []*pb.Entry{{Term: new(snap.GetMetadata().GetTerm()), Index: new(snap.GetMetadata().GetIndex())}}\n   236\t\treturn nil\n   237\t}\n   238\t\n   239\t// CreateSnapshot makes a snapshot which can be retrieved with Snapshot() and\n   240\t// can be used to reconstruct the state at that point.\n   241\t// If any configuration changes have been made since the last compaction,\n   242\t// the result of the last ApplyConfChange must be passed in.\n   243\tfunc (ms *MemoryStorage) CreateSnapshot(i uint64, cs *pb.ConfState, data []byte) (*pb.Snapshot, error) {\n   244\t\tms.Lock()\n   245\t\tdefer ms.Unlock()\n   246\t\tif i <= ms.snapshot.GetMetadata().GetIndex() {\n   247\t\t\treturn nil, ErrSnapOutOfDate\n   248\t\t}\n   249\t\n   250\t\toffset := ms.ents[0].GetIndex()\n   251\t\tif i > ms.lastIndex() {\n   252\t\t\tgetLogger().Panicf(\"snapshot %d is out of bound lastindex(%d)\", i, ms.lastIndex())\n   253\t\t}\n   254\t\n   255\t\tms.snapshot = pb.EnsureSnapshot(ms.snapshot)\n   256\t\tms.snapshot.Metadata.Index = new(i)\n   257\t\tms.snapshot.Metadata.Term = new(ms.ents[i-offset].GetTerm())\n   258\t\tif cs != nil {\n   259\t\t\tms.snapshot.Metadata.ConfState = proto.Clone(cs).(*pb.ConfState)\n   260\t\t}\n   261\t\tms.snapshot.Data = data\n   262\t\treturn proto.Clone(ms.snapshot).(*pb.Snapshot), nil\n   263\t}\n   264\t\n   265\t// Compact discards all log entries prior to compactIndex.\n   266\t// It is the application's responsibility to not attempt to compact an index\n   267\t// greater than raftLog.applied.\n   268\tfunc (ms *MemoryStorage) Compact(compactIndex uint64) error {\n   269\t\tms.Lock()\n   270\t\tdefer ms.Unlock()\n   271\t\toffset := ms.ents[0].GetIndex()\n   272\t\tif compactIndex <= offset {\n   273\t\t\treturn ErrCompacted\n   274\t\t}\n   275\t\tif compactIndex > ms.lastIndex() {\n   276\t\t\tgetLogger().Panicf(\"compact %d is out of bound lastindex(%d)\", compactIndex, ms.lastIndex())\n   277\t\t}\n   278\t\n   279\t\ti := compactIndex - offset\n   280\t\t// NB: allocate a new slice instead of reusing the old ms.ents. Entries in\n   281\t\t// ms.ents are immutable, and can be referenced from outside MemoryStorage\n   282\t\t// through slices returned by ms.Entries().\n   283\t\tents := make([]*pb.Entry, 1, uint64(len(ms.ents))-i)\n   284\t\tents[0] = &pb.Entry{Index: new(ms.ents[i].GetIndex()), Term: new(ms.ents[i].GetTerm())}\n   285\t\tents = append(ents, ms.ents[i+1:]...)\n   286\t\tms.ents = ents\n   287\t\treturn nil\n   288\t}\n   289\t\n   290\t// Append the new entries to storage.\n   291\t// TODO (xiangli): ensure the entries are continuous and\n   292\t// entries[0].Index > ms.entries[0].Index\n   293\tfunc (ms *MemoryStorage) Append(entries []*pb.Entry) error {\n   294\t\tif len(entries) == 0 {\n   295\t\t\treturn nil\n   296\t\t}\n   297\t\n   298\t\tms.Lock()\n   299\t\tdefer ms.Unlock()\n   300\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
