{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_50","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_13-42-19-etcd_raft-real-run/agent-source && cat -n raftpb/raft.proto'","aggregated_output":"     1\tsyntax = \"proto2\";\n     2\tpackage raftpb;\n     3\t\n     4\toption go_package=\"go.etcd.io/raft/v3/raftpb\";\n     5\t\n     6\tenum EntryType {\n     7\t\tEntryNormal       = 0;\n     8\t\tEntryConfChange   = 1; // corresponds to pb.ConfChange\n     9\t\tEntryConfChangeV2 = 2; // corresponds to pb.ConfChangeV2\n    10\t}\n    11\t\n    12\tmessage Entry {\n    13\t\toptional uint64     Term  = 2;\n    14\t\toptional uint64     Index = 3;\n    15\t\toptional EntryType  Type  = 1;\n    16\t\toptional bytes      Data  = 4;\n    17\t}\n    18\t\n    19\tmessage SnapshotMetadata {\n    20\t\toptional ConfState conf_state = 1;\n    21\t\toptional uint64    index      = 2;\n    22\t\toptional uint64    term       = 3;\n    23\t}\n    24\t\n    25\tmessage Snapshot {\n    26\t\toptional bytes            data     = 1;\n    27\t\toptional SnapshotMetadata metadata = 2;\n    28\t}\n    29\t\n    30\t// For description of different message types, see:\n    31\t// https://pkg.go.dev/go.etcd.io/raft/v3#hdr-MessageType\n    32\tenum MessageType {\n    33\t\tMsgHup               = 0;\n    34\t\tMsgBeat              = 1;\n    35\t\tMsgProp              = 2;\n    36\t\tMsgApp               = 3;\n    37\t\tMsgAppResp           = 4;\n    38\t\tMsgVote              = 5;\n    39\t\tMsgVoteResp          = 6;\n    40\t\tMsgSnap              = 7;\n    41\t\tMsgHeartbeat         = 8;\n    42\t\tMsgHeartbeatResp     = 9;\n    43\t\tMsgUnreachable       = 10;\n    44\t\tMsgSnapStatus        = 11;\n    45\t\tMsgCheckQuorum       = 12;\n    46\t\tMsgTransferLeader    = 13;\n    47\t\tMsgTimeoutNow        = 14;\n    48\t\tMsgReadIndex         = 15;\n    49\t\tMsgReadIndexResp     = 16;\n    50\t\tMsgPreVote           = 17;\n    51\t\tMsgPreVoteResp       = 18;\n    52\t\tMsgStorageAppend     = 19;\n    53\t\tMsgStorageAppendResp = 20;\n    54\t\tMsgStorageApply      = 21;\n    55\t\tMsgStorageApplyResp  = 22;\n    56\t\tMsgForgetLeader      = 23;\n    57\t\t// NOTE: when adding new message types, remember to update the isLocalMsg and\n    58\t\t// isResponseMsg arrays in raft/util.go and update the corresponding tests in\n    59\t\t// raft/util_test.go.\n    60\t}\n    61\t\n    62\tmessage Message {\n    63\t\toptional MessageType type        = 1;\n    64\t\toptional uint64      to          = 2;\n    65\t\toptional uint64      from        = 3;\n    66\t\toptional uint64      term        = 4;\n    67\t\t// logTerm is generally used for appending Raft logs to followers. For example,\n    68\t\t// (type=MsgApp,index=100,logTerm=5) means the leader appends entries starting\n    69\t\t// at index=101, and the term of the entry at index 100 is 5.\n    70\t\t// (type=MsgAppResp,reject=true,index=100,logTerm=5) means follower rejects some\n    71\t\t// entries from its leader as it already has an entry with term 5 at index 100.\n    72\t\t// (type=MsgStorageAppendResp,index=100,logTerm=5) means the local node wrote\n    73\t\t// entries up to index=100 in stable storage, and the term of the entry at index\n    74\t\t// 100 was 5. This doesn't always mean that the corresponding MsgStorageAppend\n    75\t\t// message was the one that carried these entries, just that those entries were\n    76\t\t// stable at the time of processing the corresponding MsgStorageAppend.\n    77\t\toptional uint64      logTerm     = 5;\n    78\t\toptional uint64      index       = 6;\n    79\t\trepeated Entry       entries     = 7;\n    80\t\toptional uint64      commit      = 8;\n    81\t\t// (type=MsgStorageAppend,vote=5,term=10) means the local node is voting for\n    82\t\t// peer 5 in term 10. For MsgStorageAppends, the term, vote, and commit fields\n    83\t\t// will either all be set (to facilitate the construction of a HardState) if\n    84\t\t// any of the fields have changed or will all be unset if none of the fields\n    85\t\t// have changed.\n    86\t\toptional uint64      vote        = 13;\n    87\t\t// snapshot is non-nil and non-empty for MsgSnap messages and nil for all other\n    88\t\t// message types. However, peer nodes running older binary versions may send a\n    89\t\t// non-nil, empty value for the snapshot field of non-MsgSnap messages. Code\n    90\t\t// should be prepared to handle such messages.\n    91\t\toptional Snapshot    snapshot    = 9;\n    92\t\toptional bool        reject      = 10;\n    93\t\toptional uint64      rejectHint  = 11;\n    94\t\toptional bytes       context     = 12;\n    95\t\t// responses are populated by a raft node to instruct storage threads on how\n    96\t\t// to respond and who to respond to when the work associated with a message\n    97\t\t// is complete. Populated for MsgStorageAppend and MsgStorageApply messages.\n    98\t\trepeated Message     responses   = 14;\n    99\t}\n   100\t\n   101\tmessage HardState {\n   102\t\toptional uint64 term   = 1;\n   103\t\toptional uint64 vote   = 2;\n   104\t\toptional uint64 commit = 3;\n   105\t}\n   106\t\n   107\t// ConfChangeTransition specifies the behavior of a configuration change with\n   108\t// respect to joint consensus.\n   109\tenum ConfChangeTransition {\n   110\t\t// Automatically use the simple protocol if possible, otherwise fall back\n   111\t\t// to ConfChangeJointImplicit. Most applications will want to use this.\n   112\t\tConfChangeTransitionAuto          = 0;\n   113\t\t// Use joint consensus unconditionally, and transition out of them\n   114\t\t// automatically (by proposing a zero configuration change).\n   115\t\t//\n   116\t\t// This option is suitable for applications that want to minimize the time\n   117\t\t// spent in the joint configuration and do not store the joint configuration\n   118\t\t// in the state machine (outside of InitialState).\n   119\t\tConfChangeTransitionJointImplicit = 1;\n   120\t\t// Use joint consensus and remain in the joint configuration until the\n   121\t\t// application proposes a no-op configuration change. This is suitable for\n   122\t\t// applications that want to explicitly control the transitions, for example\n   123\t\t// to use a custom payload (via the Context field).\n   124\t\tConfChangeTransitionJointExplicit = 2;\n   125\t}\n   126\t\n   127\tmessage ConfState {\n   128\t\t// The voters in the incoming config. (If the configuration is not joint,\n   129\t\t// then the outgoing config is empty).\n   130\t\trepeated uint64 voters = 1;\n   131\t\t// The learners in the incoming config.\n   132\t\trepeated uint64 learners          = 2;\n   133\t\t// The voters in the outgoing config.\n   134\t\trepeated uint64 voters_outgoing   = 3;\n   135\t\t// The nodes that will become learners when the outgoing config is removed.\n   136\t\t// These nodes are necessarily currently in nodes_joint (or they would have\n   137\t\t// been added to the incoming config right away).\n   138\t\trepeated uint64 learners_next     = 4;\n   139\t\t// If set, the config is joint and Raft will automatically transition into\n   140\t\t// the final config (i.e. remove the outgoing config) when this is safe.\n   141\t\toptional bool   auto_leave        = 5;\n   142\t}\n   143\t\n   144\tenum ConfChangeType {\n   145\t\tConfChangeAddNode        = 0;\n   146\t\tConfChangeRemoveNode     = 1;\n   147\t\tConfChangeUpdateNode     = 2;\n   148\t\tConfChangeAddLearnerNode = 3;\n   149\t}\n   150\t\n   151\tmessage ConfChange {\n   152\t\toptional ConfChangeType  type    = 2;\n   153\t\toptional uint64          node_id = 3;\n   154\t\toptional bytes           context = 4;\n   155\t\n   156\t\t// NB: this is used only by etcd to thread through a unique identifier.\n   157\t\t// Ideally it should really use the Context instead. No counterpart to\n   158\t\t// this field exists in ConfChangeV2.\n   159\t\toptional uint64          id      = 1;\n   160\t}\n   161\t\n   162\t// ConfChangeSingle is an individual configuration change operation. Multiple\n   163\t// such operations can be carried out atomically via a ConfChangeV2.\n   164\tmessage ConfChangeSingle {\n   165\t\toptional ConfChangeType  type    = 1;\n   166\t\toptional uint64          node_id = 2;\n   167\t}\n   168\t\n   169\t// ConfChangeV2 messages initiate configuration changes. They support both the\n   170\t// simple \"one at a time\" membership change protocol and full Joint Consensus\n   171\t// allowing for arbitrary changes in membership.\n   172\t//\n   173\t// The supplied context is treated as an opaque payload and can be used to\n   174\t// attach an action on the state machine to the application of the config change\n   175\t// proposal. Note that contrary to Joint Consensus as outlined in the Raft\n   176\t// paper[1], configuration changes become active when they are *applied* to the\n   177\t// state machine (not when they are appended to the log).\n   178\t//\n   179\t// The simple protocol can be used whenever only a single change is made.\n   180\t//\n   181\t// Non-simple changes require the use of Joint Consensus, for which two\n   182\t// configuration changes are run. The first configuration change specifies the\n   183\t// desired changes and transitions the Raft group into the joint configuration,\n   184\t// in which quorum requires a majority of both the pre-changes and post-changes\n   185\t// configuration. Joint Consensus avoids entering fragile intermediate\n   186\t// configurations that could compromise survivability. For example, without the\n   187\t// use of Joint Consensus and running across three availability zones with a\n   188\t// replication factor of three, it is not possible to replace a voter without\n   189\t// entering an intermediate configuration that does not survive the outage of\n   190\t// one availability zone.\n   191\t//\n   192\t// The provided ConfChangeTransition specifies how (and whether) Joint Consensus\n   193\t// is used, and assigns the task of leaving the joint configuration either to\n   194\t// Raft or the application. Leaving the joint configuration is accomplished by\n   195\t// proposing a ConfChangeV2 with only and optionally the Context field\n   196\t// populated.\n   197\t//\n   198\t// For details on Raft membership changes, see:\n   199\t//\n   200\t// [1]: https://github.com/ongardie/dissertation/blob/master/online-trim.pdf\n   201\tmessage ConfChangeV2 {\n   202\t\toptional ConfChangeTransition transition = 1;\n   203\t\trepeated ConfChangeSingle     changes =    2;\n   204\t\toptional bytes                context =    3;\n   205\t}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
